From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sinful1992 <sinful1992@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 01:13:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Persist stream tickets so a server restart does not revoke
 them

The Android TV app fetches a direct-play stream with no credentials at
all, relying on the ticket issued at PlaybackInfo. With the ticket table
in memory only, every restart of the server turned an in-progress
playback into a loop of 401s until the user pressed play again. Seen
live on 2026-09-20 01:08: a restart during an episode, then one stream
request every five seconds rejected for two minutes. Stock Jellyfin has
no such failure because it has no such check; the fork must not add one.

StreamTicketStore now writes its table to data/stream-tickets.json --
on every issue, and at most once a minute while an in-flight stream
slides a ticket -- as a temp file moved into place, and reloads it at
construction, dropping anything already expired. A corrupt or missing
file starts empty; clients renegotiate. The parameterless constructor
keeps the memory-only behaviour for tests. ApplicationHost registers the
persistent form explicitly.
